markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Variables

#### Have you ever noticed how a game keeps track of your score or how an app shows your number of likes? 

#### Programs do this using **variables!** Just like we use names to keep track of things in real life, programs use **variables** to keep track of changing information.

<div style="width:90%; margin:auto; font-family:sans-serif; font-size:14pt;">

<!-- Variable -->
<h2 style="font-size:14pt;">What is a Variable?</h2>
<div style="display:flex; align-items:center; margin-bottom:40px;">
    
<div style="flex:2; padding-right:20px;">
<p style="font-size:14pt;">A <b>variable</b> is a named container that stores a value that can change.</p>
      
<p style="font-family:monospace; font-size:12pt; background-color:#f4f4f4; padding:8px; border-radius:6px; display:inline-block;">
        score = 0
      </p>
      
<p style="font-size:14pt;">When the player earns points, the value increases.</p>
    </div>
<div style="flex:1; text-align:center;">
<img src="https://images.code.org/6aac191fc61f33592100e3d59ac7b717-image-1762187266713.png" 
           alt="Eboxes symbolizing variable storage" 
           style="width:70%; border-radius:10px;">
    </div>
  </div>

<!-- State -->
<h2 style="font-size:14pt;">What is State?</h2>
<div style="display:flex; align-items:center; margin-bottom:40px;">
    
<div style="flex:2; padding-right:20px;">
<p style="font-size:14pt;"><b>State</b> is the current values of all the variables in a program.</p>
      
   <p style="font-family:monospace; font-size:12pt; background-color:#f4f4f4; padding:8px; border-radius:6px; display:inline-block;">
        score = 10 &nbsp;&nbsp; level = 2
      </p>
      
      <p style="font-size:14pt;">Together, these values describe what is happening right now.</p>
    </div>

<div style="flex:1; text-align:center;">
      <img src="https://images.code.org/e3a6cd792666963257437c276c283ed5-image-1762187492236.png" 
           alt="Graph showing score and level as state" 
           style="width:70%; border-radius:10px;">
    </div>
  </div>

<!-- Operation -->
<h2 style="font-size:14pt;">What is an Operation?</h2>
<div style="display:flex; align-items:center;">
    
<div style="flex:2; padding-right:20px;">
<p style="font-size:14pt;">An <b>operation</b> changes a variable’s value.</p>
      
<p style="font-family:monospace; font-size:12pt; background-color:#f4f4f4; padding:8px; border-radius:6px; display:inline-block;">
        score = score + 1
      </p>
      
<p style="font-size:14pt;">This adds one to the current score.</p>
    </div>

<div style="flex:1; text-align:center;">
      <img src="https://images.code.org/2d7424f5c8832b0c3266d95848076aa7-image-1762187634384.png" 
           alt="Graph showing +1 operation changing score" 
           style="width:60%; border-radius:10px;">
    </div>
  </div>

</div>

    
<br>  

---

## Let's see what this looks like in code:

#### This code defines the variables using the keyword `let`.

#### In JavaScript, a variable must be declared, or defined, before it can be used. 

<p style="text-align:center;">
  <img src="https://images.code.org/3fc90531df0a250a6d4d032641296c12-image-1762197748560.png" 
       alt="JavaScript variable example" 
       style="width:600px; border-radius:10px; box-shadow:2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.3);">
</p>


### Here, an **event listener** increases the `strength` variable by 2 each time the **strength-up** button is clicked.


<p style="text-align:center;">
  <img src="https://images.code.org/642d6e6839a3abde291b95a4ae7258f1-image-1771971228529.png" 
       alt="Event handler example" 
       style="width:600px; border-radius:10px; box-shadow:2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.3);">
</p>

## Remember:

#### There are always two steps when working with variables:

- #### 1. Define the variable (give it a name and a starting value).

- #### 2. Use or update the variable within the program’s actions or logic.


## Let's practice! 

#### In the next level, you will use AI to help debug a program with variables. 


MARKDOWN
